# Analytic and Contemporary Philosophy: The Language Turn

> Imagine philosophy's oldest problems — truth, mind, science,
> justice — suddenly being re-asked as questions about *words*.
> That was the twentieth century's move. One half of the century
> policed language with logic (analytic philosophy); the other half
> used language to unmask power (continental thought). Both agreed
> on one thing: the old way of doing metaphysics had to change.

## 1. The Break with the Nineteenth Century

Kant to Existentialism left a fork: absolute idealism collapsed,
psychology and physics professionalised, and philosophy lost its
empire piece by piece. Two responses defined the new century —
**analytic philosophy** (Anglophone: clarity, logic, science as
model) and **continental philosophy** (European: history, culture,
critique of society). They would barely speak to each other for a
hundred years.

## 2. Frege and Russell: Philosophy Meets Logic

[definition]
**Gottlob Frege** (1848–1925), the father of analytic philosophy,
invented modern quantified logic to show that arithmetic rests on
pure logic (**logicism**). His **sense/reference (Sinn/Bedeutung)**
distinction — "morning star" and "evening star" name Venus but with
different senses — turned meaning itself into a technical problem.
[/definition]

**Bertrand Russell** pushed the programme further. His **theory of
descriptions** dissolved an ancient puzzle: "The present king of
France is bald" seems to be about nothing at all, yet we understand
it. Russell showed it secretly asserts three claims (a king exists,
he is unique, he is bald) — some false. Meaning was analysed by
logical form, not surface grammar.

## 3. Wittgenstein: Two Philosophies in One Life

Ludwig Wittgenstein is the century's singular figure — and wrote
its two most influential books that contradict each other.

| | *Tractatus* (1921) — early | *Philosophical Investigations* (1953) — late |
| --- | --- | --- |
| View of language | A picture of facts; logical form mirrors the world | A toolkit of **language games** |
| Aim of philosophy | Say only what can be said; show the rest | Dissolve confusions therapy-style |
| Slogan | "Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent" | "Meaning is use" |

The late Wittgenstein replaced the single logical language with
countless activities — asking, thanking, joking, commanding — each
with its own grammar, its own **form of life**. Philosophy's job
became untangling knots we tie ourselves into.

## 4. Logical Positivism and Its Collapse

The **Vienna Circle** (Schlick, Carnap, Neurath; A.J. Ayer its
English courier) radicalised the programme with the **verification
principle**: a statement is meaningful only if empirically
verifiable or analytically true. Ethics, metaphysics, and theology
were dismissed as *nonsense* — not false but meaningless. It was
intellectually thrilling and self-refuting: the verification
principle is itself unverifiable. Quine's **"Two Dogmas of
Empiricism" (1951)** then demolished the analytic/synthetic wall
and the myth of the isolated statement — knowledge faces experience
"as a corporate body" (**holism**).

## 5. Ordinary Language, Science, and the Post-Positivist Scene

- **Ordinary language philosophy** (Oxford: J.L. Austin, later
  Searle): meaning lives in everyday usage — Austin's *speech-act
  theory* distinguished what words *say* (locution), *do*
  (illocution), and *effect* (perlocution).
- **Philosophy of science matured:** Popper replaced verification
  with **falsifiability** (science = conjectures that risk
  refutation); **Thomas Kuhn's** *Structure of Scientific
  Revolutions* (1962) added history — normal science, **paradigms**,
  and revolutionary shifts.
- **Political philosophy revived:** John Rawls's *A Theory of
  Justice* (1971) — justice as fairness behind a "veil of
  ignorance" — restarted normative theory, answered by Nozick's
  libertarianism and communitarians (Sandel, MacIntyre).

## 6. The Continental Counter-Current

Meanwhile, continental Europe ran its own rebellion:

- **Frankfurt School** critical theory (Adorno, Horkheimer, later
  Habermas): Enlightenment reason had turned instrumental,
  serving domination — critique must rescue reason from itself.
- **Phenomenology to existentialism** (Husserl to Sartre,
  Beauvoir, Merleau-Ponty): consciousness is always consciousness
  *of* something; the body and lived experience precede theory.
- **Post-structuralism:** Foucault (knowledge/power — every
  "truth" regime disciplines), Derrida (**deconstruction** — no
  stable centre to language), Lyotard ("incredulity toward
  grand narratives").
- **Pragmatism's return** (American: Peirce, James, Dewey;
  revived by Rorty): truth is what works in the long run of
  inquiry — a third way that spoke to both camps.

[example]
Watch the method difference on one word — "power." An analytic
philosopher asks: under what conditions is the sentence "the state
has power over me" true, and what would verify it? Foucault asks:
what historical discourse made this way of speaking about bodies
and institutions possible, and whom does it serve? Same word,
different medicines — one polices the sentence, the other
historicises it.
[/example]

## 7. The Present: Naturalism and Applied Turns

Contemporary philosophy is post-split: philosophers of mind
(Dennett, Chalmers on consciousness and qualia, the "hard
problem") argue with cognitive science; bioethics and AI ethics
run hospital and tech-policy committees; experimental philosophy
polls intuitions; and analytic feminism, analytic Marxism, and
formal epistemology export the method everywhere. The newest
frontier — machine intelligence — has handed philosophers of
language and mind a wholly new interlocutor.

[summary]
Analytic philosophy began with Frege's logic and Russell's theory of
descriptions, split into Wittgenstein's two philosophies (the
Tractatus's logical picture, the Investigations' language games and
"meaning is use"), and radicalised in the Vienna Circle's
verificationism before Quine's holism toppled it. Kuhn's paradigms,
Austin's speech acts, Popper's falsification, and Rawls's veil of
ignorance rebuilt the field's science, language, and politics
wings, while the continent answered with critical theory,
existential phenomenology, and the post-structuralisms of Foucault
and Derrida. Pragmatism bridged the camps; today's naturalised,
applied, and computational philosophy — including AI ethics —
inherits both toolkits.
[/summary]
